1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,950
At the south pole of the Moon,
the Sun never climbs high.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:09,950
It stays low, near the edge of the sky.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,687
So one hill can hide it, and leave
a lander in the dark for days.

4
00:00:14,737 --> 00:00:15,950
We are [Team name].

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:19,950
I'm Wali.

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:21,950
I'm Ryan.

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:23,950
I'm Ahbab.

8
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:25,950
I'm Mehrab.

9
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:27,950
I'm Mehedi.

10
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,106
NASA pays private companies to carry
its instruments to the Moon.

11
00:00:33,156 --> 00:00:40,606
The programme is called CLPS, and our challenge
is to build a browser for these missions.

12
00:00:40,656 --> 00:00:45,762
The facts are public, but they sit
in many different places.

13
00:00:45,812 --> 00:00:49,981
A team planning a landing asks one thing first:

14
00:00:50,031 --> 00:00:54,669
can my lander work at this place, on these dates?

15
00:00:54,719 --> 00:00:57,950
Finding out takes experts, and many days.

16
00:00:58,000 --> 00:00:59,605
So we built PolarWindow.

17
00:00:59,655 --> 00:01:02,502
It puts every mission in one place.

18
00:01:02,552 --> 00:01:09,950
And for any landing place and dates, it works out
sunlight and contact with Earth, hour by hour.

19
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:14,466
Here is every mission in the programme,
flown and planned.

20
00:01:14,516 --> 00:01:18,079
Each fact links to where it came from.

21
00:01:18,129 --> 00:01:23,950
Each mission follows the same six steps,
from getting ready to coming home.

22
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,495
On the surface, four numbers matter:

23
00:01:26,545 --> 00:01:29,889
hours of sunlight, hours in contact with Earth,

24
00:01:29,939 --> 00:01:34,132
the longest night, and the longest time
with no contact.

25
00:01:34,182 --> 00:01:37,950
Each one shows how far it could be off.

26
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:42,990
Why? Because of the hills
around the landing place.

27
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:51,950
As the days play, the Sun and Earth move
along that skyline, and the numbers follow.

28
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,017
You can put landing places side by side,
one number at a time.

29
00:01:58,067 --> 00:02:02,683
If two are too close to call, it says so.

30
00:02:02,733 --> 00:02:05,950
There is no score and no winner.

31
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:11,283
A team can add its own mission
by dropping in a short document.

32
00:02:11,333 --> 00:02:16,206
A planned mission with no dates yet
still gets a worked example.

33
00:02:16,256 --> 00:02:21,950
Anything assumed is marked with stripes,
so no one takes it for a fact.

34
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,283
Or just ask a question.

35
00:02:25,333 --> 00:02:32,617
The assistant can only say numbers
that the app worked out,

36
00:02:32,667 --> 00:02:37,950
and it shows how it got each one.

37
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,719
PolarWindow runs on NASA open data.

38
00:02:40,769 --> 00:02:49,027
Laser height maps from the Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter
give the shape of the ground around each landing place.

39
00:02:49,077 --> 00:02:55,488
NASA's SPICE data gives the positions of the Sun,
the Earth and each spacecraft.

40
00:02:55,538 --> 00:03:01,488
The Deep Space Network data says
when a dish on Earth can listen.

41
00:03:01,538 --> 00:03:07,950
We also use NASA data for space weather,
the Moon's gravity and ground temperature.

42
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:09,728
Can you trust it?

43
00:03:09,778 --> 00:03:17,283
We checked our results against missions that have
already flown, using NASA's records of their real paths.

44
00:03:17,333 --> 00:03:20,839
Over fourteen hundred automatic tests guard the code.

45
00:03:20,889 --> 00:03:23,950
Every number says where it came from.

46
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,617
For teams that build landers and instruments,
the answer is now on one page.

47
00:03:30,667 --> 00:03:37,760
For students and anyone curious, a real Moon mission
is open in a web browser.

48
00:03:37,810 --> 00:03:41,093
PolarWindow is open source, and live today.

49
00:03:41,143 --> 00:03:43,950
We are [Team name]. Thank you.
